  `clear-hold` WITH NO ARGUMENT DIED: `SyntaxError: f-string: invalid syntax -> (v.get(class))`.
  The listing block sits inside a double-quoted `python3 -c "…"`, where an inner " is not a quote to
  the shell — it CLOSES the argument — so `v.get("class")` reached Python as `v.get(class)`, a
  keyword. The RELEASE branch one line below was already argv-driven and worked; BOSS proved it
  live. The listing branch is reached only with no argument and the suite never exercised it. Third
  unexercised branch of a guard to ship broken in one night (the others: `restart`'s self-call
  behind --dry-run, the render-time aging behind a stubbed write_pending).
  Both the path and the strings now come in through argv, so no shell quoting reaches Python.
