  AND THE CLASS, which is the part that matters: the test compiles EVERY embedded `python3 -c "…"`
  body in the script after applying the shell's own quote-stripping, and refuses any body containing
  a bare ". That catches this defect without anyone running the branch, and catches the next one in
  a branch nobody has run either. Its own control asserts the scan reaches every embedded Python
  present (2 of 2), because a checker that silently skips a form is the shape it exists to catch.
  DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: running every ctl verb to see which crash — `restart`, `pause` and `stop`
  act on the live daemon. The class check is static for that reason and is weaker for it: it catches
  a body that cannot parse, not one that parses and does the wrong thing.

